WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Narrator] Last year,
policymakers were convinced

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.320
that inflation would be.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.670
- Transitory.
- Temporary.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.770
- That's a transitory thing.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.330
- [Narrator] But over a year later.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.260
- I think I was wrong then about the path

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.260
that inflation would take.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.820
- [Narrator] For most of 2022,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.692
inflation has lingered above 8%,

00:00:20.692 --> 00:00:21.840
a 40-year high.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.080
It's affected everything
from gas prices to groceries.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.740
But why has it lasted longer
than many experts expected?

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.270
There are a few key factors

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.980
that propped it up and made it tricky

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.410
to predict what comes next.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.470
The first is consumers have more to spend.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.790
At the beginning of the pandemic,

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.530
emergency measures from Washington,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.990
including stimulus checks, loan pauses,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.010
low borrowing costs and more
flushed the economy with cash.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.290
- And those resources really built up

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.650
and it was a large stockpile of dry tinder

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:57.570
that was ready to spend as
the economy loosened up.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.850
- [Narrator] This is the
US personal saving rate.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.860
The amount of money
households have left over

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.950
after they spend and
pay taxes every month.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.350
This spike and the ones
that followed contributed

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.380
to $2.3 trillion in extra savings,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.810
which is one of the reasons
consumers have continued

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.750
to spend as prices have gone up.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.950
- There was pent-up demand.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.390
Humans are social animals.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.280
They wanted to go back out to restaurants,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.540
they wanted to see their friends,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.950
they wanted to visit families.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.450
It resulted in more spending

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.310
but it also resulted in companies
demanding higher prices.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.970
- [Narrator] The Fed estimated
that there was still a stock

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.970
of $1.7 trillion in excess
savings in mid-2022.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.310
And as consumers have continued spending,

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.070
companies have been hiring
to meet that demand.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.390
- There was just such a demand

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.750
for workers after the pandemic

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.450
that companies had a lot
of openings for positions

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.490
that they just couldn't fill

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.490
and an environment of
very low unemployment.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.990
- [Narrator] Here's a
chart that helps show

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.300
just how strong the job market is.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.340
This is the number of job openings,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.860
and this is the number of
people who are unemployed.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.230
- On the one hand, you
have an excess of demand

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.600
from all the money in
household bank accounts

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.930
and the other is you
have a deficit of supply

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.270
from shortages of not only workers

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.500
but also goods sourced
in global supply chains.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.480
- [Narrator] To help try to
fill those vacant positions,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.310
and encourage workers to stay on,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.770
businesses have also raised wages.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.146
Compensation for private

00:02:24.146 --> 00:02:26.520
and state and local government workers

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.220
has grown by 5% from last year.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.960
But when companies raise wages,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.810
they often raise prices
to make up the difference.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:37.260
- As wages go up, companies
respond by raising prices.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.930
But as prices go up, people
say my cost of living

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.477
is going up, you've gotta increase my pay.

00:02:42.477 --> 00:02:45.120
And this process starts feeding on itself.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.040
- [Narrator] But aside from
consumer spending power

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.150
and the labor market,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.060
inflation has just been a
really hard target to hit.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.670
- It's kind of been like
watching a game of Whac-A-Mole.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.790
Every time you see
inflation pop up in one area

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.260
and think you understand it,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.320
it puts its head and down
pops up in some other area.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.760
- [Narrator] Price increases have rippled

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.160
through the economy at different times.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.640
In 2021, prices for things
like groceries, cars

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.460
and gas were pushed up
by economic recovery

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.900
from the pandemic.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.110
Then in 2022, airline tickets rose as well

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.650
as the war in Ukraine drove
up the price of jet fuel.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.020
And as some of those
prices started to ease,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.400
housing kept going up

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.160
as the economy reopened
and rents increased.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.680
- So inflation has run
right through your home,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.480
from the stuff you put in your home,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.700
the televisions and the furniture

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.800
to the actual cost of living in the home,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.440
from the goods sector
to the services sector,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.600
right there in your own day-to-day life.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.980
- [Narrator] And that bouncing around

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.420
has made it harder for the Fed

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.390
to forecast how long
policies need to be enacted

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.130
in order to bring inflation down.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.350
- I just think that the inflation picture

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.420
has become more and more challenging

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.700
over the course of this
year, without question.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.290
That means that we have to have policy

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.870
being more restrictive

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.030
and that narrows the
path to a soft landing.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.710
- It's using this instrument

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.800
that hits certain sectors of
the economy harder than others.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.390
So it raises interest rates,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.150
which raises the cost of interest

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.530
in interest-sensitive sectors,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.990
like housing but it
might not hit other parts

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.350
of the economy where inflation is acute.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.270
- [Narrator] The International
Monetary Fund says

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.760
that interest rate increases
have their peak effect

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.550
on inflation after three to four years.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.940
- Monetary policy has
long and variable lags,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.100
which is another way
of saying it takes time

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.490
for these effects to work
their way into the economy.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.680
And the backdrop to the economy

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:35.850
is constantly changing.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.200
That's the variable part.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.270
So it takes time to play through

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.700
and how it plays through is unpredictable.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.460
So the story is still unfolding.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.350
- [Narrator] The Fed made
another interest rate increase

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.850
at the beginning of November

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.190
and said that they may
push interest rates higher

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.693
than originally expected.

